        <section id="so,-what-exactly-is-exception-handling?" >
          
            <h2>So, what exactly is Exception Handling?</h2>
              <p>Exception Handling refers to the way a program handles exceptional circumstances and continues to run in situations where it would otherwise crash. Different programming languages have different causes for errors which would cause the code to crash, as we discussed in Unit 4, Session 2. Using exception handling, if we can anticipate the specific type of error which may occur, we can code ways to deal with it rather than a terminal error occurring which causes the program to crash.</p>
<p>Lets use a simple example of some code to illustrate how we can use exception handling to prevent a program from crashing.</p>
<p>We have a function in Python that we want to add two numbers together, and then print the output to the console. As we can from the output of this example from test_1, this works fine when the input variables are both Ints.</p>

              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-python"><code class="language-python"><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token triple-quoted-string string">""" Python code with NO exception handling """</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Here we define a function to add two input values then print the answer.</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">def</span> <span class="token function">add_values_and_print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>a<span class="token punctuation">,</span> b<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  c <span class="token operator">=</span> a <span class="token operator">+</span> b</span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'The output is equal to: '</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> c<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">return</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>c<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now we test the function. First with integers:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_1 <span class="token operator">=</span> add_values_and_print<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token number">4</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now with an integer and a string:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_2 <span class="token operator">=</span> add_values_and_print<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token string">'goat'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span></code></pre>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="result" >
          
            <h2>Result</h2>
              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-text"><code class="language-text"><span class="highlight-line">The output is equal to:  6</span><br><span class="highlight-line">Traceback (most recent call last):</span><br><span class="highlight-line">  File "U4S4_Python_no_exception_handling.py", line 14, in <module></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    test_2 = add_values_and_print(2, 'goat')</span><br><span class="highlight-line">  File "U4S4_Python_no_exception_handling.py", line 6, in add_values_and_print</span><br><span class="highlight-line">    c = a + b</span><br><span class="highlight-line">TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'int' and 'str'</span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">[Finished in 125ms]</span></code></pre>
</div>

            
              <p>However, when one of the input variables is a string, it causes a ‘TypeError’ and crashes.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="anticipating-a-&#39;typeerror&#39;"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Anticipating a &#39;TypeError&#39;</h2>
              <p>If we anticipate a ‘TypeError’ occurring, we can use exception handling to ensure that the program continues to run in spite of this error, or displays a more informative error message.</p>
<p>In the following Python code, we have rewritten the code to do this using a “try/except” block which starts from line 6. This will ‘try’ the operation (adding the variables) and run the code in the exception following the occurrence of the specified ‘TypeError’ during this  operation.</p>

              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-python"><code class="language-python"><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token triple-quoted-string string">""" Python code with exception handling """</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Here we define a function to add two input values then print the answer.</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">def</span> <span class="token function">add_values_and_print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>a<span class="token punctuation">,</span> b<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">try</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span>				<span class="token comment"># This is where we try the operation</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    c <span class="token operator">=</span> a <span class="token operator">+</span> b</span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">except</span> TypeError<span class="token punctuation">:</span>	<span class="token comment"># This is where we state what to do (the exception) in case of a TypeError</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'TypeError. Please ensure that both input variables are Ints.'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">return</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">else</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'The output is equal to: '</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> c<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">return</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>c<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now we test the function. First with integers:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_1 <span class="token operator">=</span> add_values_and_print<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token number">4</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now with an integer and a string:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_2 <span class="token operator">=</span> add_values_and_print<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token string">'goat'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span></code></pre>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="result"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Result</h2>
              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-text"><code class="language-text"><span class="highlight-line">The output is equal to:  6</span><br><span class="highlight-line">TypeError. Please ensure that both input variables are Ints.</span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">[Finished in 132ms]</span></code></pre>
</div>

            
              <p>Now, when a “TypeError” occurs due to one of the inputs being a string and not a number, the program throws an error and prints a custom error message, but it continues to run through to completion rather than causing an terminal error.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
